Results of Code Analyze
# Model Overview (for Diagnosis)

The supplied code diagnoses item embeddings and positive-versus-negative scores 
through a model view. The HSTU backbone implementation is absent, so its 
internal representation computation cannot be verified. `log2feats(sequence)` 
supplies per-position sequence features; `predict_score(features, items)` 
converts those features into candidate logits. The probe scores all valid 
training positions rather than selecting a final user representation. Any final 
readout elsewhere must use the final nonpadding position for left-padded 
histories.

# Diagnosis Inputs & Hooks

`DiagnosisProbe(view, device)` stores the view and its `meta_data`, 
`review_data`, and `user_train`; the latter two are unused. It accesses 
`view.item_emb.weight` and `view.model.itemnum` to select real-item embeddings, 
excluding padding and reserved tokens.

The loader yields `(None, sequence, positive, negative)` with tensors shaped `B 
× L`. For up to ten batches, the probe switches to evaluation mode, computes 
sequence features without gradients, stacks positive and negative IDs into `B × 
L × 2`, and scores them together. Positions with positive ID zero are excluded.

# Probes & Metrics

- **Embedding collapse:** Samples up to 2,048 item embeddings using seed 42, 
normalizes them, averages off-diagonal cosine similarities, and maps the result 
through `(mean_cosine + 1) / 2`. Higher values indicate stronger directional 
alignment; approximately 0.5 corresponds to mean cosine zero.
- **Score margins:** Computes `positive_logit − negative_logit`. Higher mean and
tenth-percentile margins indicate clearer separation. Lower 
negative-beats-positive and margin-below-0.5 rates indicate fewer ranking errors
and weak separations.
- **Category breakdown:** Counts metadata category occurrences separately for 
positive and negative items in low-margin and wrong-rank cases, retaining the 
top twenty categories.

# Perturbation & Tests

There are no sequence shuffles, masking interventions, sensitivity tests, or 
gradient probes. Random embedding subsampling controls computation; sampled 
negatives provide the score comparisons.

# MODEL_DIAGNOSIS Schema & Limitations

`run_full_diagnosis` returns `metrics` and `metric_definitions`, covering 
collapse, four margin statistics, and nested category counts. 
`diagnosis_interpreter_prompt` embeds both into instructions requesting JSON 
with `status`, `core_findings`, `key_implications`, and `evidence`; it does not 
itself generate that interpretation.

These are training-sample diagnostics, not validation HR@10 or full-catalog 
quality measurements. Margins depend on logit scale and negative sampling. 
Category metadata is unavailable in this task, so category lists will normally 
be empty. Missing valid positions produce zero-valued margin defaults, which 
must not be interpreted as measured health.

